    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 161646\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n950 AM PDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Isolated showers and thunderstorms will be possible this afternoon \n  and evening across the higher terrain of southern Nevada, \n  southeastern California, and northwestern Arizona. \n\n* Temperatures will steadily increase throughout the week, with \n  areas of Moderate-to-Major HeatRisk developing across the Colorado \n  River Valley, Death Valley, and portions of the Las Vegas Valley.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Saturday.\n\nThe anomalous moisture that has helped to fuel storms over the past \nfew days, will decrease during the first half of the week as the \nupper-level flow becomes more southwesterly-to-westerly thanks to \nthe ridge building over the area. However, first, this southwesterly \nflow will help to advect mid-level moisture from Tropical Depression \nHernan into the region overnight. This moisture will bring increased \ncloud cover to the region and a 15 to 30% chance for showers and \nthunderstorms in the higher terrain of southern Nevada, San \nBernardino County, and Mohave County today. Storms today will be \nless robust than those we have seen over the past few days, with the \nprimary concerns being localized moderate-to-heavy rain, lightning, \nand erratic outflow winds. Any storms that develop will diminish \nafter sunset with the loss of daytime heating. Beyond today, \ndecreasing moisture combined with increasing large-scale subsidence \nfrom the building ridge will result in a lull in convective activity \nduring the first half of the week.\n\nAs the aforementioned ridge of high pressure builds over the area, \nwe will see temperatures increase through the first half of the \nweek, with above normal temperatures returning during the latter \nhalf of the week. Increasing temperatures will bring increasing \nHeatRisk, with widespread Moderate HeatRisk across the region by \nThursday and pockets of Major HeatRisk popping up in Death Valley, \nthe Colorado River Valley, and the Las Vegas Valley. As ridging \nbuilds over the area, ensemble guidance points at upper-level flow \nbecoming more southeasterly in northwestern Arizona, which would \nopen the door for moisture to creep back into the region. However, \nuncertainty remains high at this time, so no promises.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Winds \nare expected to remain light and generally follow typical daily \ndiurnal patterns through the TAF period. There remains a small \nchance (10-20%) for VCSH near the terminals this afternoon so \nmaintained VCSH in the TAF for now. Aside from any shower activity, \nVFR conditions will prevail into Monday with bases remaining AOA \n10kft AGL. The high temperature will approach 100F between 22Z and \n01Z.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Isolated to scattered \nconvection is expected again this afternoon, primarily across \nsouthern Clark, eastern San Bernardino, and central Mohave counties. \nWhile the current thinking is that the activity will stay out of the \nLas Vegas Valley, a few remnant showers from storms southwest of \ntown may make it into the valley. Brief rain and gusty outflow winds \nare the main concerns near any storms. Convection should decrease \nduring the evening hours. Outside of any convective influences, \nwinds in most locations should be light. The exceptions are the \nsouthern Great Basin and Owens Valley, where breezy south winds are \nforecast. Cloud bases should remain AOA 10kft AGL through the TAF \nperiod. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Stessman \nAVIATION...Planz/CO\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
